 "answer": "The collection has **two 2022 papers with linked code repositories**, both published on **December 20, 2022**. [D1][D2]\n\n1. **Precise Zero-Shot Dense Retrieval without Relevance Labels (HyDE)** — [Code](https://github.com/texttron/hyde) · arXiv:2212.10496. [D1]  \n   HyDE asks an LLM to write a hypothetical document answering your query, embeds that document, then finds real documents with similar embeddings. The generated text is a search aid—not a trusted answer—and the method needs neither relevance labels nor new model training. [C1][C2]\n\n2. **Interleaving Retrieval with Chain-of-Thought Reasoning for Knowledge-Intensive Multi-Step Questions (IRCoT)** — [Code](https://github.com/stonybrooknlp/ircot) · arXiv:2212.10509. [D2]  \n   IRCoT tackles questions that require multiple evidence-gathering steps. It retrieves initial passages, generates a reasoning step using them, uses that step to search for more passages, and repeats; the collected evidence then supports the final answer. [C10] The paper reports better retrieval and question-answering performance, plus fewer factual errors in generated reasoning, than one-step retrieval. [C7]",
